    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe addition sits naturally in `NormalizeOwnerEmail`, which already owns the email-processing responsibilities for the owner pipeline: it reads the email, trims it, runs a syntactic validity check (throwing `InvalidEmailException`), and normalizes to lowercase. A domain-blocklist rejection is the same kind of concern \u2014 input policy on the owner email \u2014 and it reuses the established mechanism (`InvalidEmailException`, which the existing escalation handling already maps to 400) rather than inventing a parallel path.\n\nSupporting observations:\n- The blocklist is a well-named `static final Set<String>` constant with a clear doc comment \u2014 the idiomatic place for it, not an inline literal.\n- The check is correctly ordered: syntactic validation first, then the domain extraction operates on the already-lowered/trimmed string, so the `Set.of(...)` lowercase entries match reliably without a second normalization pass.\n- The change is purely additive; it doesn't disturb the existing control flow or duplicate the trimming/validation logic.\n\nThe class stays cohesive (\"prepare and vet the owner's email\") rather than becoming a grab-bag, so a maintainer of this codebase would find it a natural extension of the existing step.",
